# One line ending everywhere: LF, in the repository AND in every working tree.
#
# Without this, git checks text files out with the platform's native ending (CRLF on Windows) while
# .editorconfig/dotnet format decide theirs separately — so an editor or tool that writes LF into an
# otherwise-CRLF file produces whitespace errors that `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` fails on but
# `git diff` hides, because git normalizes endings when it diffs. That is invisible in review and only
# surfaces in CI (it broke the 1.0.7 release). Pinning both sides to LF removes the mismatch and makes
# the format check give the same verdict on Windows, macOS and Linux. .editorconfig sets the matching
# end_of_line = lf; the two must agree.
#
# Every blob here is already stored with LF, so this changes no committed content — only what lands in
# a Windows working tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings on every platform (a CRLF shebang/case-statement breaks
# on Linux) — needed for JustDownload.Tests/Fixtures/fake-secret-tool.sh (TASK-113), which a Linux
# CI runner executes directly via its shebang. Covered by the rule above; kept explicit so it survives
# any later change to it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Batch files are the exception: cmd.exe has long-standing parsing quirks with LF-only files (labels
# and multi-line blocks), and these only ever run on Windows.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
